{
 "id": "l38",
 "label": "38",
 "title": "Forming nouns from verbs",
 "summary": "Gerunds: turn -um-, mag-, -in, paki-, ma- and -an verbs into nouns such as pag-alís (leaving), pagbása (reading) and paghuhúgas (washing)",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what a gerund (verbal noun) is",
  "Form nouns from -um- and -in verbs with pag- + root",
  "Form nouns from mag- and -an verbs with pag- + duplicated first syllable + root",
  "Form nouns from paki- verbs by repeating the last syllable of paki-",
  "Choose pag- (actions) or pagka- (feelings) for ma- verbs",
  "Use the new nouns with ang in a sentence"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What is a gerund?",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **gerund** is a **verbal noun**: a noun made from a verb. In English it usually ends in **-ing** (or is a phrase with an -ing verb), and it does the job of a noun in the sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Take *Writing calms my mind*. Here **writing** is a gerund. You can prove it: a pronoun can replace it in the next sentence (*It calms my mind*), and only nouns can be replaced by pronouns."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Spot the gerund",
     "items": [
      "*He left* → verb (an action).",
      "*His leaving made me sad* → gerund (a thing you can talk about).",
      "In Tagalog the gerund is a different word from the verb: [[umalís|pred]] (left) but [[pag-alís|noun]] (leaving, departure)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Lesson 31 you saw that [[ang|marker]], [[ng|marker]] and [[sa|marker]] can turn a verb or adjective into a noun. In this lesson only [[ang|marker]] is needed (or [[ang mga|marker]] if the verbal noun is plural), together with **six affixes**, one pattern for each kind of verb."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Verb type",
      "Formula for the noun",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "-um-",
       "pag- + root",
       "umalís → **pag-alís**"
      ],
      [
       "mag-",
       "pag- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "maglarô → **paglalarô**"
      ],
      [
       "-in",
       "pag- + root",
       "basáhin → **pagbása**"
      ],
      [
       "paki-",
       "paki- + duplicated last syllable of paki- + root",
       "pakisúlat → **pakíkisúlat**"
      ],
      [
       "ma-",
       "pag- + root (actions); pagka- + root (feelings)",
       "maligò → **pagligò**; magálit → **pagkagálit**"
      ],
      [
       "-an",
       "pag- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "hugasan → **paghuhugas**"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The six patterns at a glance"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "From -um- verbs: pag- + root",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Take away the **-um-** and put **pag-** in front of the root. When the root begins with a vowel, a hyphen is written after pag- (pag-alís, pag-ibig), as you learned in Lesson 2."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "pag- + root",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "umalís",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "pag-",
         "r": "other"
        },
        {
         "t": "alís",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "→ pag-alís",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "lumakad",
       "chips": [
